        "commentary": "Morning (6 AM CT, Oct 22): BTC ~$82.0K (24h $81.6K-$84.8K) after Wednesday's run at $85K reversed. It has lost the $82.5K support that analysts quoted by CoinDesk flagged, and the 1h and 4h are Sell while the daily is Neutral (under EMA20 ~$82.6K, above EMA50 ~$80.8K). The selling is macro, not crypto-specific: Nasdaq 100 futures fell after Tesla cut its spending forecast and Lam Research guided lower, China's CSI 300 entered a bear market, Brent crude topped $112 and the VIX rose to ~18 (CoinDesk). CoinGlass counted ~$431M of liquidations in 24 hours, with longs taking $210M of the last $222M. Underneath, spot BTC ETFs keep buying: +$528.4M on Oct 21 and over $1B in three sessions (The Block); Glassnode places buyers near ETF and treasury cost bases around $80K and the next sell-order cluster at $85K-87.5K. Total crypto market cap fell ~4.9% in 24h to ~$2.74T; BTC dominance ~60%; OKX funding is a flat ~0.007%. Yen watch: USD/JPY ~156.9, down from ~158.3, the yen's biggest daily rally in a month on safe-haven flows (Reuters), and the Nikkei fell 2.65% to 62,908, its first close under 63,000 since July (Kyodo). That raises carry-trade unwind risk a notch but doesn't trigger it: 155 is still about 1.2% away, and Nikkei Asia reports the BOJ will hold at 1.25% on Oct 29-30. A fast break below 155 would be the warning sign. Plan: $81.5K (today's low) and $80.1K (Oct 15 low) are the floors; a reclaim of $82.5K-83K steadies things. The T007 swing long (from $82,300, stop $80,800) stays open. Dated risks: flash PMI Oct 23, FOMC Oct 28, GDP/PCE Oct 29.",
